From a07c77010aa65edd7f6db15a8a874707fc299d9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 08:43:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/20] relay_metrics: Count tunneled directory requests and
 responses

tor_relay_streams_total{type="BEGIN_DIR"} counts BEGIN_DIR streams, but
nothing after that: whether a stream ever sent a usable request, what it
asked for, and whether the response left the directory connection.

Count each tunneled request once, as
tor_relay_begindir_request_total{type}, by what it asked for: hs_get,
hs_post, consensus, microdesc, descriptor, other_get (including those we
answer with 404), other_post (including every POST to a relay that isn't
public), unparsed (it didn't parse, was too large or had an unknown
method, or only part of it arrived before the connection timed out),
no_request if nothing arrived before the stream or the connection
closed, or circuit_dead if we dropped it, or the part of it that
arrived, unread because its stream or circuit was already closing, such
as those queued on a circuit closed for its second HSDir request. A GET
takes the type of the URL table entry that handles it. A directory
connection keeps one more than its type in four bits, so that it is
counted once, and every request we answer is counted before its answer;
one that isn't is counted when its connection closes.

Count, as tor_relay_begindir_response_total{type="hs_descriptor|other"},
the responses fully handed to their stream in
connection_dir_finished_flushing(), which does not mean the client
received them.

Only tunneled (BEGIN_DIR) connections are counted, so on a public relay
the request types sum to the accepted BEGIN_DIR streams, which
tor_relay_streams_total{type="BEGIN_DIR"} counts in total, less those
still waiting for their request. The counters live in the relay module,
with stubs for the dircommon callers. directory_handle_command_post()
grows to 138 lines: raise its practracker exception.

Part of #41339.
